<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 1. ABSTRACT                                                               -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>1. Abstract</h1>
<p>
  The modern economic landscape has witnessed an exponential proliferation of digital transactions, multiple earning channels, and fragmented payment instruments. In this dynamic context, maintaining rigorous financial health has become increasingly complex for individuals and households. Conventional methods—such as static spreadsheet templates, physical ledgers, or opaque banking apps—fail to provide real-time budget feedback, multi-year spending insights, or granular cashflow controls.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Aura Money</strong> is conceived and engineered as an intuitive, high-performance, single-page cloud application (SPA) designed to empower users with full autonomy over their financial lives. The primary objective of this project is to build a secure, responsive, and aesthetically refined platform that seamlessly unifies income tracking, expense categorization, real-time budget variance monitoring, and multi-scale visual analytics spanning monthly trajectories and multi-year comparisons.
</p>
<p>
  During this <strong>First Review Phase</strong>, the core architectural foundation and operational modules of the system have been successfully designed, implemented, and verified:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cloud State &amp; Secure Authentication:</strong> Integrated Firebase Authentication with Google Federated Single Sign-On and Cloud Firestore for resilient, owner-bound document persistence.</li>
  <li><strong>Unified Financial Ledger:</strong> Developed responsive, schema-validated entry mechanisms for recording inflows (salaries, freelance earnings, investments, recurring deposits) and outflows categorized across key living domains (Housing, Groceries, Utilities, Transportation, Entertainment, Health, and Education).</li>
  <li><strong>Real-Time Budget Engine:</strong> Implemented proactive budget controls featuring automated progress thresholds, warning indicators (&gt;90% consumption), and deficit alerts without page reloads.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-Scale Visual Analytics &amp; Reporting:</strong> Built rich data visualization suites utilizing Recharts and D3 principles, encompassing 12-month annual cashflow bar charts, cumulative net savings curves, category distribution donuts, and daily spend trajectories.</li>
  <li><strong>Dual-Scope Financial Export Subsystem:</strong> Engineered a robust, RFC 4180-compliant CSV generation engine supporting both monthly summaries and comprehensive multi-year reports with sanitized ledger records and executive summaries.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The system adheres to OWASP Top 10 security standards, zero-hardcoding credential hygiene, and responsive layout guidelines, delivering sub-second update cycles across desktop, tablet, and mobile environments.
</p>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 2. INTRODUCTION                                                           -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>2. Introduction</h1>

<h2>2.1 Background of the Project Work</h2>
<p>
  Personal financial literacy and active cashflow management are foundational pillars of individual economic stability. The transition toward cashless economies, digital wallets, unified payment interfaces (UPI), and recurring online subscriptions has radically decoupled consumer spending from physical currency awareness. Consequently, a substantial majority of individuals experience "invisible spending leakage"—untracked micropayments and lifestyle creep that steadily deplete potential savings reserves.
</p>
<p>
  Aura Money was initiated to address this operational gap by engineering a clean, privacy-centric financial management dashboard that converts raw transactional data into actionable financial clarity.
</p>

<h2>2.2 Establishing the Context</h2>
<p>
  Existing personal finance software generally falls into two extremes:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Complex, Commercial Banking Aggregators:</strong> These services demand full access to third-party bank credentials, display intrusive commercial advertisements for loans/credit cards, and present cluttered, opaque interfaces.</li>
  <li><strong>Manual Spreadsheets (Excel / Google Sheets):</strong> While customizable, spreadsheets lack automated schema validation, are cumbersome to update on mobile devices, do not provide real-time budget threshold warnings, and are prone to formula corruption and accidental data deletion.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Aura Money bridges this divide by delivering a self-hosted, cloud-synchronized web application that provides the agility of a web dashboard with the rigor of enterprise database security and instantaneous graphical feedback.
</p>

<h2>2.3 Why This Problem Is Important</h2>
<p>
  Proactive financial monitoring yields tangible socioeconomic benefits:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Deficit Prevention:</strong> Immediate feedback on category spending stops budget breaches before month-end.</li>
  <li><strong>Informed Wealth Accumulation:</strong> Tracking net savings rate over rolling 12-month periods fosters disciplined investing and emergency buffer preparation.</li>
  <li><strong>Tax and Audit Readiness:</strong> Structured itemized ledgers exportable to standardized CSV format dramatically simplify seasonal tax filings and personal audits.</li>
  <li><strong>Psychological Peace of Mind:</strong> A clear, predictable visual representation of income-to-debt ratios eliminates financial uncertainty and cognitive stress.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2.4 Development Process Followed</h2>
<p>
  The project follows an <strong>Iterative Agile / Component-Driven Development Lifecycle</strong>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Phase 1: Domain Modeling &amp; Architecture:</strong> Definition of strict TypeScript domain models, Firestore database schema rules (`firestore.rules`), and cryptographic authentication boundaries.</li>
  <li><strong>Phase 2: Core Transaction Engine:</strong> Incremental delivery of income logging, expense categorization, and date-partitioned Firestore document synchronizers.</li>
  <li><strong>Phase 3: Budgeting &amp; Real-Time Aggregation:</strong> Reactive state pipeline calculating category utilization, variance thresholds, and savings rates across memoized selectors.</li>
  <li><strong>Phase 4: Visual Analytics &amp; Reporting Suite:</strong> Integration of responsive SVG visualization components (Recharts), monthly spend curves, and annual 12-month performance grids.</li>
  <li><strong>Phase 5: Data Extraction &amp; Quality Verification:</strong> Implementation of client-side CSV sanitization (CSV Injection prevention, UTF-8 BOM encoding) and rigorous linting/build automation.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 3. ANALYSIS AND REQUIREMENTS                                              -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>3. Analysis and Requirements</h1>

<h2>3.1 Problem Analysis</h2>
<p>
  Through user research and workflow evaluations, three core problem dimensions were identified:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data Entry Friction:</strong> Users abandon financial apps if logging an expense requires more than three interactions. The interface must provide streamlined, mobile-optimized modal dialogs with smart category defaults.</li>
  <li><strong>Temporal Disconnect:</strong> Most budgeting applications treat months as isolated silos, hiding annual compounding trends. Users require both micro-scale (daily spend trajectory within the month) and macro-scale (12-month annual growth) analytical perspectives.</li>
  <li><strong>Data Portability:</strong> Users demand true ownership of their data without vendor lock-in, necessitating one-click export of uncompressed, structured financial records.</li>
</ul>

